`swage.segment_reduce` (`swage_public_call`) against `torch.segment_reduce`, with a new offsets layout on every call. Each figure is the median across processes of the ratio of the two per-process medians, and the range is over the rows of the run:

- Rank one, 8,192 segments: 1.70 to 4.44 times as long, lowest on `uniform` and highest on `log-normal`; slower in every process in every one of the 9 rows.
- Rank two, 2,048 segments, D of 3 and 64: 0.16 to 4.44 times as long, lowest on `power-law D=64` and highest on `alternating-empty D=64`; slower in every process in 4 of the 12 rows, and faster in every process in 8 of the 12 rows.
- Rank two, 8,192 segments, D of 3 and 64: 0.06 to 5.72 times as long, lowest on `power-law D=64` and highest on `alternating-empty D=3`; slower in every process in 6 of the 12 rows, and faster in every process in 6 of the 12 rows.
- Rank two, 32,768 segments, D of 3 and 64: 0.01 to 9.19 times as long, lowest on `power-law D=3` and highest on `alternating-empty D=3`; slower in every process in 4 of the 8 rows, and faster in every process in 4 of the 8 rows.
- Rank two, 2,048 segments, D of 768: 0.39 to 1.67 times as long, lowest on `power-law D=768` and highest on `alternating-empty D=768`; slower in every process in 2 of the 4 rows, and faster in every process in 2 of the 4 rows.

On int64 offsets (`swage_public_call_int64`) the call took 1.03 to 1.67 times as long as on the int32 offsets of the same layouts, over all 45 rows.
